            </p>
            <p>
                In this project, a Nexys video FPGA was used to implement the system shown below. The FPGA intercepts the HDMI signal coming from
                a laptop, or other HDMI source, crops the image based on user inputs, resizes the cropped video into a known format,
                then tries to classify the result as a number using the K-Means algorithm, trained using the MNIST data set.
                

                <br>  <br>
                
                Although this is not a particularly useful system, this project is interesting as it involves working with 
                a high resolution video stream and provides a low latency, high frame rate means of digit classification. The dynamic resizing IP block was also non-trivial to implement
                and with some modifications could have uses in other applications.
                


            </p>
            <p><img src="/imgs/classification_all.png" alt="img" width="50%" height="auto"></p>

            Features:
            <ul>
                <li>The input HDMI video stream is resized to a 28x28 video stream based on the sub-box selection of the user. This is done using the custom dynamic resizing IP.</li> <br>
                <li>This 28x28 frame is captured in an SRAM buffer for a short time while the K-Means algo is run.</li> <br>
                <li>The final classification, and the resized image, are both captured by the MicroBlaze processor, and shown on the OLED screen. All at 60fps. </li> <br>
                <li>HDMI is allowed to pass through to a monitor/HDMI sink. A box is imprinted on the screen to show what region the user has selected. </li>
            </ul>
            
            
            <br>



            
            Main Design Components:
                <ul>
                    <li>HW dynamic resizing IP -> See <a class=goodlinks href="/resizing/resizing_implementation">Dynamic HW Video Resizing IP Implementation</a></li>
                    <li>K-Means classification (HLS). Trained in software using MNIST data set</li>
                    <li>Box control via push buttons </li>
                    <li>MicroBlaze FW - Loop to capture 28x28 SRAM buffer, capture K-Means result, display both on the OLED display</li>
                    <li>Software mode - Extra option to run all HW features in SW instead to show the improvement made by using HW. </li>
                    <li>HDMI I/O + datapath</li>
                </ul>
